IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clwa-dates`
IMPORT `clwa-liability`

-- Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), liability, defences and damages -- worked cases for the questions of the person
-- bringing a claim for personal injury or death.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the text before the file was run; none was copied from the encoding's output. All facts are HYPOTHETICAL.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Short names`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`on` d m y MEANS `the calendar date` d m y

`ANY DAY` MEANS `on` 1 1 2000

§ `s 5 -- good samaritans`

`PROTECTED` MEANS `yes: the good samaritan incurs no personal civil liability for this (s 5 (1))`
`NOT A PROTECTED ACT` MEANS `no: s 5 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, by a good samaritan as s 5 (4) defines one (the text's no)`

#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PROTECTED`
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT A PROTECTED ACT`
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT A PROTECTED ACT`
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the protection does not apply where the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme (the text's no, s 5 (2) (a))`
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the protection does not apply where their capacity was significantly impaired by a recreational drug (the text's no, s 5 (2) (b))`
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: naloxone given honestly and without recklessness for an opioid overdose is protected even if they were impaired by a recreational drug (s 5 (3))`
-- s 5 (3) overrides only s 5 (2) (b), not the CTP exclusion.
#ASSERT `s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the protection does not apply where the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme (the text's no, s 5 (2) (a))`

§ `ss 8, 9 -- volunteers`

`THE ORGANISATION ONLY` MEANS `the community organisation: the volunteer's liability attaches to it, and you may not sue the volunteer personally (s 8 (1), s 9 (1), (2))`
`THE VOLUNTEER, CLEARLY UNPROTECTED` MEANS `the volunteer personally: it is clear the s 8 protection does not apply (s 8 (2), s 9 (2) (a))`

#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `THE ORGANISATION ONLY`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the community organisation (s 9 (1)), and also the volunteer personally, because the organisation disputed in its filed defence that it is liable (s 9 (2) (b))`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `THE VOLUNTEER, CLEARLY UNPROTECTED`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `THE VOLUNTEER, CLEARLY UNPROTECTED`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `THE VOLUNTEER, CLEARLY UNPROTECTED`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `THE VOLUNTEER, CLEARLY UNPROTECTED`
#ASSERT `s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the volunteer personally: s 8 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, in community work for a community organisation on a voluntary basis (the text's no)`

§ `s 14 -- apologies`

#ASSERT `s 14 -- does the apology count as an admission` FALSE EQUALS `nothing as to fault: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding them, and cannot be used in evidence of them (s 14)`
#ASSERT `s 14 -- does the apology count as an admission` TRUE EQUALS `part 2.3 does not apply to defamation, Discrimination Act 1991 or Workers Compensation Act 1951 claims (the text's no, s 12 (2)); this Act says nothing more about the apology (NOTHING-kind)`

§ `s 16 -- what the estate can recover`

`PAIN` MEANS `pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm, or loss of expectation of life`
`EARNINGS` MEANS `loss of capacity to earn, or loss of future probable earnings`
`RECOVERABLE` MEANS `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`
`EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH` MEANS `no: where the wrong caused the death, these damages are not included (the text's no, s 16 (3))`

#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `exemplary damages` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: exemplary damages are not recoverable in a surviving cause of action (the text's no, s 16 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `exemplary damages` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: exemplary damages are not recoverable in a surviving cause of action (the text's no, s 16 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `PAIN` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH`
-- Mesothelioma, notice of claim given before death, died of it: s 16 (4).
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `PAIN` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: for an asbestos-related disease, notified under s 51 before death, that caused or contributed to the death (s 16 (4))`
-- The same, but no notice before death: excluded.
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `PAIN` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH`
-- The wrong did not cause the death (the person died of something else): recoverable.
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `PAIN` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `RECOVERABLE`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `EARNINGS` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH`
-- s 16 (4) lifts only (3) (b) (i), not lost earnings.
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `EARNINGS` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `EARNINGS` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `RECOVERABLE`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `loss of capacity to perform domestic services (s 100)` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `EXCLUDED FOR A DEATH`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `reasonable funeral expenses paid or payable by the estate` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: reasonable funeral expenses may be included, though the court may reduce them having regard to the dead person's age and health (s 16 (5), (6))`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `reasonable funeral expenses paid or payable by the estate` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `where the wrong did not cause the death, funeral expenses are not a loss the wrong caused; s 16 (5) is not tied to s 16 (3) in its words, and is read here (fork F13) as giving no answer (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `other damages (for example medical expenses or earnings lost before death)` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `RECOVERABLE`
#ASSERT `s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `other damages (for example medical expenses or earnings lost before death)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: causes of action for defamation do not survive under part 2.4 (the text's no, s 15 (2))`

§ `s 23 -- members of the family`

#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `their domestic partner`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `their child, grandchild or stepchild (including one born alive after the death)`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `someone they were acting in place of a parent to, immediately before the death`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `their parent, step-parent or grandparent`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `someone who was acting in place of a parent to them, immediately before the death`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `their brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister`
#ASSERT `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `their former spouse, civil union partner or civil partner`
#ASSERT (`s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `someone else (for example an aunt, uncle, cousin, niece, nephew, step-sibling or friend)`) EQUALS FALSE

§ `s 26 -- payments not deducted`

`NOT DEDUCTED` MEANS `no: it must not be taken into account to reduce damages for the death (the text's no, s 26)`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `money paid under a contract of insurance on the death or injury` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `money from a superannuation, provident or similar fund, or a friendly society, benefit society, lodge or trade union` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `a payment instead of leave, or a pension or other social security or veterans benefit, paid because of the death or injury` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `a gratuity paid on the death` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `a house, or household contents, acquired by a family member after the death, used as their home` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `life insurance premiums that would have become payable had they lived` EQUALS `NOT DEDUCTED`
#ASSERT `s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or workers compensation)` EQUALS `s 26 does not list it, and this Act does not say whether it reduces the damages (NOTHING-kind)`

§ `ss 34, 35 -- mental harm`

`PURE` MEANS `pure mental harm (not from a bodily injury to me)`
`CONSEQUENTIAL` MEANS `consequential mental harm (from a bodily injury to me)`
`ECONOMIC` MEANS `economic loss (for example lost earnings or treatment costs)`
`NON-ECONOMIC` MEANS `non-economic loss (for example pain and suffering)`
`NOT BARRED` MEANS `the Act does not bar them: a duty of care can arise and s 35 does not stop these damages (ss 34, 35)`

#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `PURE` `ECONOMIC` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `NOT BARRED`
#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `PURE` `NON-ECONOMIC` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: damages for pure mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (1))`
#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `CONSEQUENTIAL` `ECONOMIC` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: damages for economic loss for consequential mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `CONSEQUENTIAL` `NON-ECONOMIC` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `NOT BARRED`
#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `PURE` `NON-ECONOMIC` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no duty of care: a reasonable person would not have foreseen a recognised psychiatric illness in a person of normal fortitude in your position (the text's no, s 34 (1))`
-- Not foreseeable for a person of normal fortitude, but the defendant knew the claimant was more vulnerable (s 34 (4)).
#ASSERT `s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `PURE` `NON-ECONOMIC` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `NOT BARRED`

§ `s 36 -- nervous shock of relatives`

`EXTENDS` MEANS `yes: the wrongdoer's liability includes your mental or nervous shock (s 36 (1)), subject to part 7.1 and to ss 34, 35`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `their parent (including a step-parent, grandparent, or someone acting in place of a parent)` FALSE EQUALS `EXTENDS`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `their domestic partner, as one of the family` FALSE EQUALS `EXTENDS`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `their child (including a grandchild, stepchild, or someone they acted in place of a parent to)` TRUE EQUALS `EXTENDS`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `their child (including a grandchild, stepchild, or someone they acted in place of a parent to)` FALSE EQUALS `s 36 reaches other family members only if it happened within their sight or hearing; it does not extend the liability to you, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `their brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister, as one of the family` TRUE EQUALS `EXTENDS`
#ASSERT `s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `not a family member (for example a friend, a cousin, or a bystander)` TRUE EQUALS `s 36 does not reach someone who is not a family member, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

§ `ss 37, 38 -- terrorism and motor vehicles`

`NO RIGHT OF ACTION` MEANS `no: there is no right of action for death or injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism (the text's no, s 38 (1))`
`NOT STOPPED` MEANS `s 38 does not stop it: not an injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism`
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NO RIGHT OF ACTION`
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes against the person who committed or promoted the act of terrorism: s 38 (1) does not remove their liability (s 38 (2) (a))`
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT STOPPED`
-- Firearms: s 37 (1) (b) need not be met (s 37 (3)).
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `NO RIGHT OF ACTION`
-- Firearms but no political, religious or ideological cause: not an act of terrorism (fork F11).
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `NOT STOPPED`
#ASSERT `s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT STOPPED`

§ `s 43 -- precautions`

#ASSERT `s 43 -- was the failure to take precautions negligent` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `chapter 4 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 41 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 43 -- was the failure to take precautions negligent` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 43 does not stand in the way: the risk was foreseeable and not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions (s 43 (1))`
#ASSERT `s 43 -- was the failure to take precautions negligent` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not negligent: one of the s 43 (1) conditions is not met (the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 43 -- was the failure to take precautions negligent` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not negligent: one of the s 43 (1) conditions is not met (the text's no)`

§ `s 94 -- offences`

#ASSERT `s 94 -- is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951, so s 94 does not exclude it (the text's no, s 93 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 94 -- is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: liability for damages is excluded (s 94 (1))`
#ASSERT `s 94 -- is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `excluded, but the court may still award damages: the case is exceptional and the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly (s 94 (2))`
#ASSERT `s 94 -- is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 94 excludes liability only where both s 94 (1) conditions are met (the text's no)`

§ `ss 95-97 -- presumptions of contributory negligence`

`PRESUMED` MEANS `yes: contributory negligence is presumed, and your damages must be reduced as far as the court considers just and equitable`
`REBUTTED` MEANS `presumed, but rebutted: you have shown what the section allows, so no presumption stands`
`NOT PRESUMED` MEANS `not presumed under this section (the text's no): the defendant must prove any contributory negligence in the ordinary way`
`NOT PART 7.1` MEANS `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 93 (2))`

-- s 95: aged 16 exactly (the edge), intoxicated, contributory negligence claimed, no rebuttal.
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 15 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 30 FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
#ASSERT `s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` TRUE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PART 7.1`

-- s 96: passenger of a drunk driver.
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 19 TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 19 TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 19 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 15 TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 19 TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
#ASSERT `s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` FALSE 19 TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `REBUTTED`

-- s 97: arguments are (wc) (motor) (age) (seatbelt) (helmet) (compartment) (less serious) (could not fasten) (could not avoid).
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 16 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
-- No helmet, and it contributed: no rebuttal is provided, so "could not reasonably have avoided the risk" does not help (finding 7).
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `REBUTTED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
-- Seatbelt ground rebutted, but no helmet as well: still presumed.
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE FALSE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 15 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` FALSE TRUE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PRESUMED`
#ASSERT `s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` TRUE TRUE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `NOT PART 7.1`

